update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('cc4e194f-f4e9-424b-be73-95aeab57cff7'::uuid,'fa6be30574c3f53da4ef7963a7afc3e7','A fires only when a project is "necessary in order to strengthen a town’s economy". The road is necessary for the complex, but nothing says the complex is the only way to strengthen Sierra''s economy, so we can''t show the road is necessary for a stronger economy. The trigger isn''t established, and A never touches the benefit to every resident that the developer argues from.'),
('e636d107-674a-4d83-b787-a8759401d561'::uuid,'57d1d5960f5612c703cf83eba95ef442','This is the tempting one, because it talks about results that "benefit all of those residents", which is exactly what the developer established. But read the direction. B says residents "should not vote in favor" of the tax unless everyone benefits. That makes benefit to all a requirement for a yes vote:

a yes vote is acceptable → the project benefits all residents

The developer has the right side, not the left. Meeting a requirement only keeps a yes vote from being ruled out. It never tells residents they should vote yes, and that is the conclusion we need.'),
('2d7ca2e6-f98d-4209-8c90-4c1711e5249c'::uuid,'b87e64ca5dfd026e058a31379961d486','C only applies once a town is funding a project, and then it compares a tax with debt. The developer''s question is whether residents should back this tax at all. C never mentions benefit, so the developer''s evidence can''t trigger it, and its outcome is about the form of funding, not about how residents should vote.'),
('a0427e64-bdc2-4188-aaf7-f936532ea28d'::uuid,'edf5416403ade790868f037db3b45bf9','D is about who "should be required to pay" a tax. The developer''s conclusion is about how residents should vote, not who gets billed. And D only limits payment to beneficiaries. Knowing everyone benefits makes everyone eligible to pay, but being eligible to pay is not a reason anyone should vote for the tax.'),
('d3717755-0ca2-4195-8c2f-c561393018c1'::uuid,'5d415f38b0a60b15839bac522713cd9f','E is the If Evidence, then Conclusion principle we predicted. Its left side, anyone "who would benefit from the results of a construction project", matches the developer''s evidence: the road is required for the complex to be finished, and the complex strengthens the economy and benefits every Sierra resident. Its right side, "should vote in favor of a local tax to fund that project", is the conclusion almost word for word.

The move to repeat: when the conclusion says someone should do something and the premises only describe facts, look for the principle whose left side is built from the premises and whose right side is the should. E supplies the missing bridge from benefit to duty.'),
('5e1c2178-b2a4-43de-8216-f660c339b094'::uuid,'836d2110b9bc5ac397efa96ed993fa4b','A gets the owner wrong. Graham''s premise is that a person is truly happy only when doing something. The sleep claim contradicts that, so it can''t be part of Graham''s argument. It belongs to the philosopher, who uses it against Graham.'),
('da9f92be-86de-48fd-9e87-99bd4d68b0fe'::uuid,'5cc9425ee7527cf57edada617e5fe9bb','B names both the form and the target. It is an example: a sleeper who is truly happy while doing nothing is one specific case. And it aims to show that "a premise of Graham’s argument is false": the case breaks Graham''s premise that true happiness requires doing something.

The move to repeat on Role questions: when a speaker attacks someone else''s argument, ask which part the attack lands on, the premise or the conclusion. Then match the answer to that exact target.'),
('bd8ada57-4c6f-4193-8a68-f5ab5d4c2734'::uuid,'2cacd371239db9d4bd5c50b08545a4cb','Graham never mentions sleep. The sleeping case is the philosopher''s own, and the philosopher relies on it rather than rejecting it. It isn''t an analogy either: it is a direct case of happiness without activity, not a comparison to some other situation.'),
('7844db72-b887-4859-974d-70f891c4496b'::uuid,'801fbca4a7cd255361407a0889422485','D is the closest trap, because the claim is an example and it is aimed at Graham. But it hits the premise, not the conclusion. A happy sleeper doesn''t show that the best life isn''t full of activity; a busy life could still be best for other reasons. That''s why the philosopher concludes only that we shouldn''t be persuaded, not that Graham''s conclusion is false.'),
('89868e79-7413-4018-ac7d-e749b9f1a4f0'::uuid,'a34d4e1f25b666ea8ab9f6438d058436','The main conclusion is that we should not be persuaded by Graham''s argument. Ask why we should believe that. Because people are sometimes truly happy while sleeping. The sleep claim answers the why, so it is support for the conclusion, not the conclusion itself.'),
('a34288b4-5159-4d0c-a53a-3bd05394722a'::uuid,'837fa0e9720228da2dfc71825dfe9a09','That''s the prediction. The claim about individuals is offered as a reason, and the conclusion depends on it: once individuals gain nothing from the variation, the remaining explanation is selection among whole species.

The move to repeat: find the conclusion by its indicator, then ask of the target claim whether it answers why we should believe the conclusion. If it does, it is support, no matter what contrast word introduces it.'),
('135f0d2e-36cd-4a0d-a7d0-a49079a211da'::uuid,'6c1a59d51c968a07f1f95bec56ddc9de','B reads the "However" as a turn against the conclusion. The turn is between two premises: good for the species, but not for individuals. Test it: if individuals did benefit, the conclusion would get weaker, because individual selection could then explain the spread. So the claim helps the conclusion. It doesn''t cast doubt on it.'),
('f9498e5b-3939-4dbf-a8fd-d195f6a59c35'::uuid,'c02b396e671e53ef60ef609d552cd2c2','The argument never questions this claim. It asserts it and builds on it. A claim the argument is designed to call into question would be something the biologist argues against, and the biologist argues from this one.'),
('70556a57-0376-4693-a86c-8707e38f9ce4'::uuid,'47772dcc6155ea5a1e4abe1e41d6b106','The phenomenon the biologist explains is that sexual reproduction "has become the rule among both animals and plants". The target claim isn''t that phenomenon. It is a fact about who benefits from genetic variation, used to help explain the spread.'),
('65f6ca62-fd4f-4734-aa9c-a1cfec939d47'::uuid,'e96f8e385b3fd3373457c4f77f35cb6e','The explanation the argument offers is in the conclusion: natural selection favoring some entire species over others. The target claim helps rule out the rival, individual-level explanation, which is why the species-level one wins. Helping to establish an explanation is not the same as being the explanation.'),
('8d033779-3696-428c-ae6c-7a7bde049f12'::uuid,'23f8efa6f85abeedd5d7a43bb81a3515','The phenomenon the argument explains is that emotion seems nonrational. An example of that would be a case of someone treating an emotion as nonrational. The joy claim is nothing like that. It is a case of language being too general, which is part of the explanation, not part of what gets explained.'),
('a0b5a2cd-f751-4f48-bd3b-b4bb155fc508'::uuid,'3eb366b74ef1986cb8a6da9afa2e4f28','The main conclusion is "emotion is not nonrational", right after the "But". The joy claim is offered to help support it, so it can''t be it. "Thus" is the bait. Here it shows the joy claim follows from the general point about emotion words, and "for example" shows it illustrates that point. A main conclusion is where the argument ends up, and this sentence is a step on the way.'),
('37d7c7c7-8c87-4a63-a9f0-2ea11de04a38'::uuid,'9daf533b8d5ede28670ff486bee2deed','C traces the support chain exactly. One joy versus another is "a specific instance", a single case. It is "illustrating a general claim," the claim that emotion words name only very general kinds of experience. And it is "thereby indirectly supporting the conclusion", because that general claim backs the point about language, which backs the conclusion that emotion isn''t nonrational.

The move to repeat: when a role answer says indirectly, check that a middle claim sits between the target and the conclusion. Here one does, so indirectly is accurate.'),
('8158db88-a746-453d-919e-560913eabc03'::uuid,'5f87afbbae6af4f3390aac50e34a4de2','A concession grants something to the other side. The other side says emotion is nonrational. The joy claim grants nothing to that view. It supports the philosopher''s account of why emotion only seems nonrational, so it works against the opposing view.'),
('ae774761-9405-4f8c-aae8-2ffb7d287b36'::uuid,'5ea3e6f535b60b0fde5d245daa6dbd38','E is tempting because the joy claim sits close to the explanation. But the explanation offered for why emotion seems nonrational is that "language lacks the ability to convey adequate conceptions of emotion." The joy claim is one case under the general point about emotion words, which in turn backs that explanation. It illustrates the support. It isn''t the explanation itself.'),
('c7d35b39-1bfc-4de0-87a3-2e104027f7f2'::uuid,'0b0a8310c82df2f854b4ca70ec9c5d34','Here is the intermediate conclusion in disguise. The claim is "a premise" because it supports the main conclusion, and "another premise is offered as support" because the disease, injury and diet sentence backs it up.

The move to repeat: on Role questions, run two checks on the target claim. Does something support it? Does it support something? Two yeses means an intermediate conclusion, whatever words the answer uses for it.'),
('2309c2af-33c1-407c-9db0-5a56ed88f5a3'::uuid,'5430292b2560d671569a277270a153d1','B says the claim "plays no logical role". Remove it and see what happens. The second sentence would be left with nothing to prove, and the conclusion would lose the hardship that makes the benefits worth mentioning. A claim that holds the argument together is not background.'),
('137068ef-561e-4692-946a-0472854e8cbf'::uuid,'3c3a98f7fc73b3a195257eb4dfdf3822','C is the trap for anyone who stopped after the first sentence. It gets half right: the claim does act as a premise for the conclusion. The failure is the second half. Ask why we should believe the transition was traumatic. The very next sentence answers: more disease and injury, and a diet lacking vital nutrients. That is support, so "no support is offered" is false.'),
('262a2098-25fa-4aaf-b88f-0f98301f3ba8'::uuid,'3fab44ddc35e7eef820ce8abe7632732','The conclusion of the argument as a whole comes after "Thus": groups that switched were likely motivated by the benefits of settled life. The traumatic claim is a step toward that point. It is a conclusion, but only an intermediate one.'),
('4b49ee3b-01e7-4ede-a0bc-82c964d16ac4'::uuid,'de0311942ff114150f960f71443330ab','Nothing in the argument pushes back on the traumatic claim. The anthropologist supports it with evidence and then builds on it. A claim the argument seeks to rebut would be followed by reasons it is wrong, and here every reason points the other way.'),
('e0842bba-1d53-46f3-9204-57f323947806'::uuid,'15994e61d39f7a6c19a55de97bc66378','A invents a conclusion. The scholar never mentions police officers or how many a legal system needs. The stated conclusion is about limiting the number of prohibited behaviors, and the first sentence supports that. An answer built on a conclusion the argument never draws can''t describe a role in it.'),
('80fde9a1-da9f-4b95-874b-de8908d7d75d'::uuid,'dd632561971b61c1e32f0c125b79b768','The conclusion comes after "Therefore": a successful legal system prohibits only a few intolerable behaviors. Ask whether anything supports the first sentence. Nothing does. It is a starting point, and a claim with nothing behind it can''t be the conclusion.'),
('0a2393ea-33c1-485b-8567-efda25259459'::uuid,'8e6b4d2571a78b072a06fa64a1e4fd76','C matches the prediction. The first sentence starts the chain: laws deter through threatened punishment, the threat needs believable punishment, more prohibitions make punishment less likely, so prohibit only the intolerable. Nothing earlier props it up.

The move to repeat: two questions settle most Role answers. Is the claim supported? Does it support something? Here the answers are no and yes. That combination is a plain premise.'),
('177722f5-bd22-435e-8307-d52f47016af0'::uuid,'4ab30c2ee471d7bdf030f1fa2bc35ee9','D is the trap for students who expect Role questions to ask about an intermediate conclusion. An intermediate conclusion has both jobs: it gets support and gives support. The first sentence gives support but gets none. The next two sentences don''t argue for it; they build on it. With no support coming in, it is a premise.'),
('7a92755a-a4a4-4103-becd-31d45bd5bc0c'::uuid,'d6be72f4b844daec49d29db01f297be2','The scholar doesn''t attack this view. The scholar builds on it. If the argument were designed to discredit the idea that laws deter by threatening punishment, we''d see reasons against it. Instead, the rest of the argument depends on it being true.'),
('414e6fb6-4645-4924-8005-1c4c8456e7ee'::uuid,'2add4c88d16eaaf6a5712d2365f60643','A aims the first sentence at the wrong claim. The water claim isn''t argued for. It is reported as something most people already know. The first sentence teams up with it instead: people care only about the most obvious problems, and right now the obvious one is water, so no grassroots push on air pollution. It supports the conclusion, not the water claim.'),
('5197c74f-24c3-4dd6-9fbf-a31228dac97e'::uuid,'edf2d4d05e733156788588f349898d9b','B names the target and the destination. The first sentence is a general rule about attention, and the argument applies it: people care only about the most obvious health problems, water is the problem people are aware of, so air pollution won''t draw a grassroots effort now. The first sentence is a premise for the "Hence" claim.

The move to repeat: when an argument pairs a general rule with a specific fact, the rule is usually a premise the conclusion applies. Check where the rule is aimed, and match the answer to that claim.'),
('e7ac4fd7-cf43-4c09-a4d7-fe66b31bf742'::uuid,'1d23678b0fe257ed6ed5294846ad0b16','C says the first sentence explains why people are aware of the water problem. The argument never asks why. It states the awareness as a fact and uses it. The first sentence is about what people pay attention to, and the argument aims it at the conclusion about air pollution controls.'),
('84acef38-3552-428e-8ce2-a5404e51f4bb'::uuid,'b532651db367f308ca9fe8d0688b7347','The "indisputable evidence" belongs to the ozone claim in the second sentence, about danger to severe asthmatics. The first sentence is about what people notice, not about whether ozone is dangerous. It offers no evidence about ozone at all.'),
('d53c9305-2adb-4c5f-af10-c5a74d60ed52'::uuid,'69679fa87c7fd4bc1339fc7db40a36d6','The main conclusion follows "Hence": a widespread grassroots effort for stricter air pollution controls is unlikely now. Ask why we should believe that. Partly because people notice only the most obvious problems. The first sentence answers the why, so it is support, not the conclusion.'),
('5da3443f-3eb6-4e64-a6ef-4b252ac6448e'::uuid,'b9c5629f980e46186a9de9b0d7f0ae6c','The main conclusion is the first sentence: our mastery of the ancient languages is imperfect. Ask why we should believe it, and the Plato''s Academy line is part of the answer. A claim that answers the why for another claim is support, not the main conclusion.'),
('7674ffe2-0c4d-4aa2-801b-6ffac58f9777'::uuid,'6f7e8559a74753d6faa07045b34fd632','B describes the claim''s job piece by piece. There is "a contrast": modern-language students can immerse themselves, and students of ancient languages can''t. The claim points it up "by example": the Plato''s Academy line makes the second side concrete with one trip nobody can take. And it is a contrast "from which the conclusion is drawn": imperfect mastery follows from it.

The move to repeat: abstract role answers are easiest to check in pieces. Match each phrase to a specific part of the passage, and reject the answer if any piece has nothing to land on.'),
('f0948b2b-235f-4636-89fa-967e5a12bcd5'::uuid,'7fc1efe7b52600eabf543599bdfbcdbb','C says the claim has "no logical relation" to the conclusion. Remove it and see. The second sentence would describe only what modern-language students can do, with nothing to compare against. The contrast that supports the conclusion would disappear, so the claim is doing logical work.'),
('7745c34e-bc87-45bb-ad27-c20d86e952be'::uuid,'e481e43a10b6178045a4032d096a311c','D starts well, because the claim is support. The problem is "guarantees the truth". Even granting that you can''t visit Plato''s Academy, the conclusion needs more: that immersion, or something like it, is needed for nearly perfect knowledge. The modern-language sentence only says the best students may get there through immersion. The claim helps the conclusion. It doesn''t force it.'),
('9625d1bf-cd58-4e5a-b56d-3612b62f0c42'::uuid,'cfa12194c7ab1401a82d5852a8c0547a','An ancillary conclusion would be a claim the argument supports on its way to the main point. Nothing in the passage argues that you can''t travel back in time. It is offered as plain fact. A claim with nothing supporting it can''t be a conclusion of any kind.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;